      <div class="wire-picker"><label for="wire-select">Trace an individual conductor</label><select id="wire-select"><option value="">Choose a wire…</option>{% for connection in explorer.connections %}<option value="{{ connection.from }}" data-basic="{{ 'true' if connection.basic else 'false' }}">{{ connection.signal }} · {{ connection.from }}</option>{% endfor %}</select><p id="wire-description" hidden></p></div>
    </aside>
    <div class="explorer-body">
  <section id="comparison-key" hidden class="comparison-note" aria-live="polite"><h2>What the full-featured examples add</h2><p>Orange identifies parts added to the basic USB 2.0 example; shared parts stay in place. Electric blue identifies your selection. Switch between cables to compare the same view.</p><dl class="comparison-facts"><div><dt>Basic USB-C</dt><dd>USB 2.0 · up to 480 Mbps · 3 A · no native display paths</dd></div><div><dt>Thunderbolt 4 reference</dt><dd>Four high-speed pairs · 40 Gbps in TB4 operation · 5 A SPR · display capable</dd></div><div><dt>Thunderbolt 5 reference</dt><dd>Four high-speed pairs · PAM-3 · 80/80 or 120/40 Gbps · 5 A EPR</dd></div></dl><p>Thunderbolt 4 and 5 share the displayed signal paths. Faster signalling and component ratings distinguish these examples; orange does not indicate changes between those two models.</p><p>The e-marker, local supply and bypass capacitor also appear in this full-featured example. An e-marker is not exclusive to Thunderbolt, and higher charging ratings do not require faster data wiring.</p></section>
  <section class="comparison-note" aria-live="polite"><h2 id="comparison-title">Inside the Thunderbolt 4 example</h2><p id="comparison-description">{{ explorer.cables.thunderbolt.description }}</p><p id="difference-note" hidden>The blue pairs, purple sideband paths and identity chip are additions to this particular basic USB 2.0 example. E-markers are also used in other USB-C cables. Shielding and other construction details can also differ.</p></section>
  <section class="comparison-note"><h2>A cable’s label is not always its physical limit</h2><p>Thunderbolt 4 operation is limited to 40 Gbps. Compatible passive cables originally qualified for 40 Gbps can also carry Thunderbolt 5’s faster PAM-3 signalling. Both endpoints must support the newer mode; a Thunderbolt 4 port remains limited to 40 Gbps. Active cables contain signal electronics, so this should not be assumed for every active TB4 cable.</p><p>An older 40 Gbps e-marker identity alongside an 80 Gbps negotiated link is therefore not, by itself, evidence of misreporting. <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/central-libraries/us/en/documents/2023-09/thunderbolt-5-technology-brief.pdf">Intel explains compatibility with existing passive cables in its Thunderbolt 5 technical brief.</a></p></section>
  <section class="comparison-note chain-explanation">
    <h2>A cable is one part of the connection</h2>
    <p>WhatCable reads the information your Mac exposes about the port, cable, connected device or charger, and negotiated connection. For displays, it also examines the monitor and DisplayPort link. It compares those reports and uses available hub and Thunderbolt topology to distinguish the link to your dock from devices further down the chain.</p>
    <p><strong>For example:</strong> an 80 Gbps port connected to a Thunderbolt 4 device is limited to 40 Gbps by that device, even if the passive cable can carry faster signalling. Replacing only the cable will not make that device run faster. WhatCable’s diagnostic accounts for that rather than blaming the cable.</p>
    <p><strong>For displays:</strong> WhatCable Pro’s Display Diagnostics compares the current resolution and refresh rate, the monitor’s reported modes and the DisplayPort link. It accounts for Display Stream Compression (DSC) and reported adapters, helping explain why a display is running at a particular mode and what to check next.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="connection-guide">
    <h2>Follow the connections</h2>
    <p>Plug 1 is shown in 3D. This table identifies the contact at the opposite plug. A and B identify contact rows, not the direction you insert the plug.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Signal</th><th scope="col">Plug 1</th><th scope="col">Plug 2</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    {% for connection in explorer.connections %}<tr data-connection="{{ connection.part }}" data-from="{{ connection.from }}" data-basic="{{ 'true' if connection.basic else 'false' }}"><th scope="row">{{ connection.signal }}</th><td>{{ connection.from }}</td><td>{{ connection.to }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    <tr><th scope="row">Outer shield</th><td>Shell + ground</td><td>Shell + ground</td></tr>
    <tr data-basic="false"><th scope="row">Coaxial shields</th><td>Ground bus</td><td>Ground bus</td></tr>
    <tr data-basic="false"><th scope="row">VCONN (local)</th><td>B5 → local e-marker / Ra</td><td>B5 → local e-marker / Ra</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </section>
  <p class="model-note">Base wiring reference: USB-C Release 2.0, Tables 3-10 / 3-11 and Figure 4-42. The Thunderbolt 5 example extends this with PAM-3 signalling and EPR power requirements. The full-featured plug has 22 contacts; B6 and B7 are absent. The USB 2.0 example includes only its connected contacts. The cable uses coaxial shielding rather than separate drain wires. Opening the view separates layers and routes for inspection. Colours identify functions; distances, component blocks and PCB trace positions are not manufacturing dimensions. Power ratings require a compatible charger and device. Wiring alone does not establish Thunderbolt certification or signal-integrity compliance.</p>
  <details class="text-guide"><summary>Read the component guide without 3D</summary>{% for part in explorer.parts %}<h2>{{ part.name }}</h2><p>{{ part.summary }} {{ part.detail }}</p><p><a href="{{ part.sourceUrl }}">{{ part.sourceLabel }}</a></p>{% if part.whatcable %}<p><strong>How WhatCable helps:</strong> {{ part.whatcable }}</p>{% endif %}{% if part.whatcableDetail %}<p>{{ part.whatcableDetail }}</p>{% endif %}{% endfor %}<h2>Thunderbolt 5: what changes</h2><p>{{ explorer.cables.thunderbolt5.description }}</p>{% for id, part in explorer.cables.thunderbolt5.partOverrides %}<h3>{{ id | capitalize }}</h3><p>{{ part.summary }} {{ part.detail }}</p><p><a href="{{ part.sourceUrl }}">{{ part.sourceLabel }}</a></p>{% endfor %}</details>
  <section id="reference-sources" class="reference-sources"><h2>How to read this reference model</h2><p>The electrical connections follow USB-C Release 2.0: §3.4 and Tables 3-10 / 3-11 define the cable wiring; §4.9 and Figure 4-42 describe the two-e-marker arrangement. Coaxial construction and that e-marker arrangement are permitted choices, not the only ways to build a cable. Colours, spacing and component blocks support inspection and are not manufacturing dimensions. Thunderbolt performance also requires its own compliance testing.</p><p>Thunderbolt 5 signalling follows Intel’s technology brief; its EPR power example uses the higher voltage and marking requirements described by UL Solutions. This does not mean every Thunderbolt 4 cable is certified for Thunderbolt 5. Each component’s expanded details link to the relevant specification section. The table above names both ends of each electrical connection.</p></section>
  <p class="explorer-sources">Learn more: <a href="https://www.usb.org/sites/default/files/USB%20Type-C%20Spec%20R2.0%20-%20August%202019.pdf">USB-IF cable specification</a> · <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/architecture-and-technology/thunderbolt/overview.html">Intel’s Thunderbolt guide</a> · <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/central-libraries/us/en/documents/2023-09/thunderbolt-5-technology-brief.pdf">Thunderbolt 5 technical brief</a> · <a href="https://www.ul.com/insights/240w-usb-c-cable-performance-testing-and-safety-considerations">EPR cable requirements</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
